  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nUpper level trough is swinging through the region this morning,\nwith a cold front now stretching east to west across northwest\nWI, and pushing southward. Strong northwest winds behind the\nfront are advecting cold air southward with temperatures in the\nlow 20s now across far northern MN. Expect temperatures to\nbottom out in the mid to upper teens for most of northeast MN \nby sunrise, with low to mid 20s elsewhere. \n\nJust enough low level moisture remains behind the front for\nscattered snow showers and horizontal convective snow showers.\nNorthwest winds are leading too inland lake effect enhancement\nas well as anthropogenic activity. Heavier snow showers could\nbriefly drop visibilities down to around 1 mile, with snowfall\nrates just heavy enough to cover roadways. There is also better\nenhancement in the snowbelt across northwest Wisconsin, with \nanother 1-3 inches over Iron County possible. The snowfall and\ngusty winds could bring blowing snow and lower visibilities for\nthe morning commute, and will leave the Winter Advisory in place\nfor Iron County through 10am. \n\nWind chills for the morning commute and school bus time will be\nin the single digits to lower teens for all of the Northland.\nBundle up if you must be outside. Snow chances taper off after\nsunrise, lingering through mid morning over Iron County, as \ndrier air pushes into the area and winds weaken. Temperatures\ntoday will only warm into the mid 20s to lower 30s. Surface high\npressure slides to our southwest tonight, with light winds with\npartly clouds skies. Lows will be in the teens Tuesday morning. \n\nFor mid week, the jet stream pattern aloft will feature split \nflow and, in general, disorganization. We may get a shortwave \ntrough passing through going into Wednesday, but without much \nmoisture to work with, we may just end up with more clouds. \nThursday is looking pretty sunny with some drier air passing \nthrough, then maybe some more clouds Friday. Our weather will be\nrelatively boring compared to a nor'easter that's looking to \nimpact the east coast Thursday into Friday. \n\nAn atmospheric river will impact Washington/Oregon around the\nsame time, and we might get some residual moisture from that\nspilling into our region for the weekend. We may get a low\npressure system moving in from the west that could bring some\nperiods of rain and snow. As of now, nothing appears significant\nin terms of rain or snow, but it may be a bit more of an active\nweather pattern in general. Precipitation type may depend a lot\non what time of day precipitation is passing through, as we may\nbe warm enough to be above freezing during the day.",
